steps.body:
The built-in collections are generic classes. `new Map<string, number[]>()` maps strings to arrays of numbers, and its `get` returns `number[] | undefined`, because a key may be missing. `new Set<string>()` holds unique strings. Given starting entries, TypeScript infers the type arguments from them:

```typescript
const stock = new Map([["mug", 4], ["kettle", 0]]);
//    ^? const stock: Map<string, number>
console.log(stock.get("mug") ?? 0); // 4
```

Node's `EventEmitter`, from `node:events`, is generic too. Its type argument maps each event name to a tuple of the arguments its listeners receive. With that map, `emit` checks the arguments you send, and each listener's parameters are inferred from the tuple:

```typescript
import { EventEmitter } from "node:events";

const files = new EventEmitter<{ saved: [file: string, bytes: number] }>();
files.on("saved", (file, bytes) => {
  console.log(`${file}: ${bytes} bytes`); // notes.txt: 512 bytes
});
files.emit("saved", "notes.txt", 512);
```

Named tuple elements, like `file: string`, label the arguments in the editor's hints.

examples.code:
const results: [student: string, score: number][] = [
  ["Ana", 91],
  ["Ben", 78],
  ["Ana", 85],
  ["Caro", 88],
  ["Ben", 82],
];

const scores = new Map<string, number[]>();
for (const [student, score] of results) {
  const list = scores.get(student);
  //    ^? const list: number[] | undefined
  if (list === undefined) {
    scores.set(student, [score]);
  } else {
    list.push(score);
  }
}
for (const [student, list] of scores) {
  console.log(`${student}: ${list.join(", ")}`);
}

